## Linux Command-Line Operations

Domain: Linux Administration | Cybersecurity | System Troubleshooting
Level: Intermediate to Advanced
Environment: Parrot OS | Kali Linux | Debian-based Systems
Focus: System Enumeration, Process Management, Permissions, Networking, Storage, Logs, and Shell Operations

---

1. Introduction

Linux command-line proficiency is an essential skill in system administration, penetration testing, digital forensics, and cybersecurity operations.

Advanced Linux usage involves more than memorizing commands. It requires understanding how processes, filesystems, permissions, network interfaces, services, and system logs interact.

This documentation covers practical commands for inspecting system state, diagnosing problems, managing resources, analyzing files, and automating administrative tasks.

2. System Enumeration

System enumeration establishes the current operating-system environment, kernel version, hardware resources, and available storage.

2.1 Operating System Information

cat /etc/os-release
uname -a
hostnamectl

Command breakdown:

- "cat /etc/os-release" — Displays distribution identification information.
- "uname -a" — Displays kernel and system information.
- "hostnamectl" — Displays or manages hostname and related system settings.

2.2 Hardware and Resource Information

lscpu
free -h
lsblk
df -hT

Command| Purpose
"lscpu"| Displays CPU architecture and processor information
"free -h"| Displays memory usage in human-readable units
"lsblk"| Lists block devices and their relationships
"df -hT"| Displays filesystem capacity, usage, and type

2.3 System Uptime and Load

uptime
top

"uptime" displays system uptime, logged-in users, and load averages.

"top" provides a live view of processes, CPU consumption, and memory usage.

Load averages represent runnable and certain uninterruptible tasks averaged over time. They should be interpreted alongside CPU count and actual system utilization.

