5. Process Management

A process is a running instance of a program. Linux assigns each process a process identifier (PID).

5.1 Process Enumeration

ps aux
ps -ef
pgrep -a ssh

- "ps aux" provides a detailed process listing.
- "ps -ef" displays processes in a different standard format.
- "pgrep -a ssh" searches for processes matching "ssh" and displays their command lines.

5.2 Process Relationships

pstree -p
ps -o pid,ppid,user,%cpu,%mem,cmd -p PID

"pstree -p" displays process relationships and PIDs.

The second command displays selected details for a specific PID. Replace "PID" with an actual process identifier.

The parent process ID (PPID) helps identify which process created another process.

5.3 Terminating Processes

kill PID
kill -TERM PID

"SIGTERM" requests that a process terminate gracefully.

If a process does not respond, "SIGKILL" can force termination:

kill -KILL PID

Use forced termination only when necessary. It prevents the process from performing normal cleanup.

5.4 Resource Monitoring

top
ps aux --sort=-%cpu
ps aux --sort=-%mem

These commands help identify processes consuming significant CPU or memory.

High resource usage should be investigated using process behavior, workload expectations, and relevant logs before concluding that the process is malicious.

