6. Services and Systemd

Systemd manages services and other system units on many Linux distributions.

6.1 Service Status

systemctl status ssh
systemctl is-active ssh
systemctl is-enabled ssh

- "status" displays service state and recent information.
- "is-active" checks whether the service is currently active.
- "is-enabled" checks whether it is configured to start through the applicable enablement mechanism.

Service names vary by distribution.

6.2 Service Management

sudo systemctl restart ssh
sudo systemctl stop ssh
sudo systemctl start ssh

These commands restart, stop, or start the selected service.

Restarting a network or remote-access service can interrupt active sessions. Confirm the service and its operational role before changing its state.

6.3 Service Logs

journalctl -u ssh
journalctl -u ssh --since "1 hour ago"
journalctl -p warning

"journalctl" queries the systemd journal.

- "-u" filters by unit.
- "--since" restricts the time range.
- "-p warning" filters for warning priority and more severe messages.

Use the service name appropriate to the installed distribution.

