7. Linux Networking and Socket Analysis

Network inspection is essential for diagnosing connectivity and understanding which services are exposed.

7.1 Interface and Address Information

ip addr show
ip route
ip link show

- "ip addr show" displays interface addresses.
- "ip route" displays routing-table entries.
- "ip link show" displays network-interface information and link state.

7.2 Active Connections and Listening Ports

ss -tuln
ss -lntp

"ss -tuln" displays listening TCP and UDP sockets using numerical addresses and ports.

"ss -lntp" displays listening TCP sockets and process details where permitted.

Important options:

- "-t" — TCP.
- "-u" — UDP.
- "-l" — Listening sockets.
- "-n" — Numerical addresses and ports.
- "-p" — Associated processes where available.

A listening port indicates that a service is waiting for connections. It does not automatically mean that the service is reachable externally.

7.3 Connectivity Testing
ping -c 4 192.0.2.10
ip route get 192.0.2.10

The IP address "192.0.2.10" is an example address reserved for documentation.

"ping" tests ICMP reachability when permitted. A failed ping does not necessarily mean that a host is offline because ICMP may be filtered.

"ip route get" shows how the kernel would route traffic to a destination.

7.4 DNS Resolution

getent hosts example.com
dig example.com

"getent hosts" resolves a hostname using the system's configured name-resolution mechanisms.

"dig" queries DNS and provides detailed response information when the utility is installed.

