10. Shell Pipelines and Text Processing

Shell pipelines connect commands so that one command's output becomes another command's input.

10.1 Pipeline Example

ps aux | grep '[s]sh'

This filters the process listing for entries matching "ssh".

The bracket expression helps avoid matching the "grep" process itself in the usual case.

10.2 Sort and Filter

ps aux --sort=-%mem | head

Displays the beginning of a process listing sorted by descending memory usage.

grep -Rni "TODO" ~/Projects

Searches project files recursively for "TODO", showing line numbers and ignoring case.

10.3 Count Results

find ~/Documents -type f | wc -l

Counts the number of regular-file paths returned by "find".

10.4 Extract Fields

cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd

Extracts the first colon-separated field from each line of "/etc/passwd", typically the account name.

10.5 Redirect Output
command > output.txt
command >> output.txt
command 2> error.log

- ">" redirects standard output and overwrites the destination file.
- ">>" appends standard output.
- "2>" redirects standard error.

Use redirection deliberately because ">" can overwrite existing data.

